    "title": "Police shoot suspect allegedly armed with knife in northeast Albuquerque - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Alika Medina ,\n19 hours ago\nALBUQUERQUE, N.M. — Albuquerque police shot a 37-year-old man near Jefferson and Osuna on Tuesday night, leaving him in critical condition in the department’s fourth shooting in less than two weeks.\nAlbuquerque Police Chief Cecily Barker said officers shot Benjamin Siegling near a Circle K.\n“We want the community to know that we are looking into these officer involved shootings, we want to remain transparent, and we will provide information as it Is available,” said Barker.\nBarker said officers were already in the area Tuesday afternoon because they had seen an increase in aggravated assaults there and were focusing on crime near the store.\n“This evening an officer did use their firearm, and shot an individual, that individual is currently at the hospital in critical condition,” said Barker.\nBarker said officers tried to talk to Siegling while they worked in the area, but he ran away.\nBarker said Siegling held a knife and walked toward an officer, who told him several times to drop it before firing at least one shot.\nBarker said the multi-agency task force is investigating the shooting.\nWhen asked about a recent spike shootings with officers, Barker said she has concerns and wants the department to move up its officer-involved shooting review.\n“I am concerned, I have already talked with the deputy chief. We usually have and OIS review every six months, we are going to move that up so we can look at any trends that we’re seeing within the police department and what we can do to address that,” said Barker.\nMedina reported Siegling is in his 30s, has a criminal history, and had two felony warrants out for his arrest.\n2",
    "summary": "Albuquerque Police Chief Cecily Barker said officers shot Benjamin Siegling near a Circle K. “We want the community to know that we are looking into these officer involved shootings, we want to remain transparent, and we will provide information as it Is available,” said Barker. Barker said officers were already in the area Tuesday afternoon because they had seen an increase in aggravated assaults there and were focusing on crime near the store. Barker said Siegling held a knife and walked toward an officer, who told him several times to drop it before firing at least one shot.",
